    "result_text": "Full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The change is complete.\n\n## What I did\n\nConsolidated the three separate duplicate checks into one derived `identityKey`, and made a full-key match the only 409 condition.\n\n**New, small, cohesive units** (each a single trivial method, no surrounding class complexity):\n- `IdentityKey.of(owner)` \u2014 builds `normalizedTelephone + \"|\" + (email or empty) + \"|\" + householdId`. Reuses the existing `HouseholdId` and the already-normalized telephone/lower-cased email, so no normalization logic is duplicated.\n- `EnsureOwnerIdentityUnique` \u2014 the single pipeline step: rejects only when a new owner's whole `IdentityKey` equals an existing owner's, throwing the new checked `DuplicateIdentityException` (with its handler + escalation YAML) that responds 409.\n\n**Rewiring / edits (kept minimal):**\n- `owners.POST.yml`: the three steps `ensureUniqueTelephone \u2192 ensureUniqueEmail \u2192 ensureUniqueHousehold` collapse to one `ensureUniqueIdentity`.\n- `OwnerMapper` + `openapi.yml`: added `identityKey` as a derived read-only field on the `Owner` response (mirroring how `householdId` is exposed), satisfying \"Return identityKey\".\n- `AcceptanceTest`: the old stub asserted a shared *telephone alone* was a conflict, which contradicts the new rule (different name/address \u21d2 different `householdId` \u21d2 different key). I corrected it to assert (a) a full-copy collision is 409, and (b) same household with a different telephone is allowed \u2014 the exact behaviour change the spec calls out.\n\nI didn't touch `BuildOwner`, `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, or any of the other pipeline steps, and I left no existing complex method grown \u2014 every changed/added function is CC 1 with essentially no surrounding class complexity.\n\nOne note: I left the now-unreferenced old check classes (`EnsureOwnerTelephoneUnique`/`Email`/`Household` and their exceptions/handlers) on disk rather than deleting them \u2014 they're inert (no route references them) and keeping the touched-file count low was the lower-impact choice. Say the word if you'd prefer them removed for a fully clean tree.",
